{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 36, :comments ["<b>Interest and increase.</b> The Rabbis treated them as one (Bava Metzia 60b), and one transgresses two negative commandments thereby — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 25:36). According to its plain sense, it appears to me that neshech is when one lends him a mina to give him in its interest five shekels each year. And it is called thus because 'his end bites like a serpent' (אחריתו כנחש ישך) — it swells and mounts. The tarbit is when one lends him until a certain time, and then he collects from him and gives him interest of five shekels, and there is no further interest after that time, for this is not neshech, since it does not increase beyond that fixed amount, but it is ribbit. Therefore Scripture says (Leviticus 25:37): 'Your money you shall not give him with neshech, and with marbit you shall not give him your food,' for the practice is to lend money with neshech — each year he pays accordingly — whereas the food one lends him until the harvest, and then he collects from his granary with a fixed addition."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:00:32.939925214Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}